## Shell confinement decisions

- Unrestricted default roots preserve the direct shell path and existing behavior.
- Restricted launches go through a dedicated Beaver helper which re-reads and canonicalizes the current roots immediately before confinement.
- macOS uses a deny-by-default Seatbelt profile. Allowed roots and a private per-process temporary directory are writable; required system/tool roots are read-only.
- Linux uses Landlock ABI V3 with a hard requirement and `no_new_privs`. ABI V3 was selected because it covers truncate operations while supporting more kernels than newer ABIs.
- Windows uses a unique AppContainer profile per shell. Allowed roots and the private temporary directory receive writable access; executable/PATH roots receive read/execute access. ACLs and the profile are removed after normal exit, forced cancellation, or startup recovery.
- Shell children inherit the confinement, so `bash_control` controls the same isolated process rather than a second unrestricted path.
- Login-profile capture and explorer subagent commands also use the sandbox, closing preparatory-shell bypasses.
- Network behavior is intentionally unchanged; this change confines local filesystem access without removing shell capabilities.
- If the OS cannot apply the sandbox, the individual launch fails closed with a generic error. It never falls back to an unrestricted process.

## Regression review

- Access narrowing/widening races were checked: the helper revalidates current settings, narrowing cancels active processes, and expansion leaves existing narrower processes safe until their next command.
- Sandbox temporary directories are private, bounded, uniquely named, and cleaned on success, spawn failure, cancellation, timeout, crash recovery, and application startup.
- Windows cleanup records live outside the sandbox-writable child directory, are size/count/path validated, and are removed even when invalid.
- Read-only tool roots are bounded and cannot overlap configured writable roots in a way that broadens a selected project to one of its parents.
- No configuration cache was added. This avoids stale-root invalidation risk; disk-read performance remains the explicitly deferred question from the review.

